steps.body:
BFS naturally moves one level at a time. To act once per level, read the queue size before the inner loop. Exactly that many cells belong to the current level, and everything they push belongs to the next one.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;
    q.push({0, 0});
    q.push({2, 5});
    int level = 0;
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "levels: " << level << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
while (!q.empty()) {
    const std::size_t levelSize = q.size();  // fixed before the loop
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < levelSize; ++i) {
        auto [r, c] = q.front();
        q.pop();
        // push unvisited neighbors
    }
    ++level;
}
```

Saving the size first matters: `q.size()` changes with every pop and push, so using it as the loop condition would mix the current level with the next one.

**Multi-source BFS** starts from several cells at once. Push every source with distance 0 before the loop starts. The waves then spread together, and each cell is reached first by its nearest source, so `dist` holds the distance to the nearest one. It costs the same as one BFS, not one BFS per source. A grid of chars can also be its own visited marker: change `.` to `F` when you push. Cells that are never reached keep their old value, so check for them afterwards when that matters.

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

const int DR[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
const int DC[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> forest = {
        "F...#.",
        "..#...",
        "....#F",
        "#.....",
    };
    const int rows = static_cast<int>(forest.size());
    const int cols = static_cast<int>(forest[0].size());

    std::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;
    for (int r = 0; r < rows; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c < cols; ++c) {
            if (forest[r][c] == 'F') {
                q.push({r, c});
            }
        }
    }

    int minutes = 0;
    while (!q.empty()) {
        const std::size_t levelSize = q.size();
        int burned = 0;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < levelSize; ++i) {
            auto [r, c] = q.front();
            q.pop();
            for (int d = 0; d < 4; ++d) {
                const int nr = r + DR[d];
                const int nc = c + DC[d];
                if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols && forest[nr][nc] == '.') {
                    forest[nr][nc] = 'F';
                    q.push({nr, nc});
                    ++burned;
                }
            }
        }
        if (burned > 0) {
            ++minutes;
            std::cout << "Minute " << minutes << ": " << burned << " cells\n";
        }
    }
    std::cout << "All burned after " << minutes << " minutes\n";
    return 0;
}

