steps.body:
BFS works on anything you can describe as **states** joined by **moves** that each cost one step. A state can be a number, a word, or a whole puzzle position. The neighbors of a state are the states one move away, and BFS finds the fewest moves to the goal.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const int start = 3;
    const int limit = 10;
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "front: {" << q.front().first << ", " << q.front().second << "}\n";
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::vector<bool> visited(limit + 1, false);  // one flag per state
std::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;            // {state, moves so far}
visited[start] = true;
q.push({start, 0});
```

When the states are whole numbers in a known range, `std::vector<bool>` indexed by the number is a compact visited set: it packs one bit per state. As with the flood fill's `visited` grid, index it directly and never bind `auto&` to its elements.

The catch is that a state space can be infinite: `x * 2` keeps growing forever. So **bound it**. Work out a limit that a shortest answer never needs to pass and skip any move that leaves the range. Check the bound before you index `visited`, because reading past the end is undefined behavior. The bound also limits memory: the vector and the queue hold at most `limit + 1` states.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

// Fewest moves from start to target using x + 1 and x * 2 (start <= target).
int fewestMoves(int start, int target) {
    const int limit = target;  // both moves only grow, so bigger values are dead ends
    std::vector<bool> visited(limit + 1, false);
    std::queue<std::pair<int, int>> q;  // {value, moves so far}
    visited[start] = true;
    q.push({start, 0});
    while (!q.empty()) {
        auto [x, moves] = q.front();
        q.pop();
        if (x == target) {
            return moves;
        }
        const int nextValues[] = {x + 1, x * 2};
        for (int next : nextValues) {
            if (next <= limit && !visited[next]) {
                visited[next] = true;
                q.push({next, moves + 1});
            }
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << "3 to 10: " << fewestMoves(3, 10) << '\n';
    std::cout << "1 to 20: " << fewestMoves(1, 20) << '\n';
    std::cout << "5 to 5: " << fewestMoves(5, 5) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

